.TH MPM 1 "2026-10-29" "Mojt Linux" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mpm \- manage Mojt Ports and inspect local native Kernel content
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mpm
.RI [ global-options ] " ports " action " " [ arguments ]
.br
.B mpm
.RI [ --root " DIRECTORY" ] " kernel " install|status|plan " " [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
MPM is Mojt's experimental package manager. The Nix-backed
.B ports
area manages shared command-line applications retained side by side in the Nix store.
Imports use binary substitutes only; unavailable substitutes fail instead
of starting source builds. System services and desktop integration are not
automatically configured for installed applications.
.PP
The repository frontend also installs exact local native Kernel packages,
inspects their retained registry and checks explicit read-only deployment plans.
These operations require no optional provider backend and never activate a Kernel.
.PP
Catalog selection, recorded requests, installed artifacts and active command
selections are separate. An artifact identity from
.B list --exact
identifies an installed build; it is not itself an upgrade request.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --root " DIRECTORY"
Dedicated state directory; default /var/lib/mojt/ports.
Use a new empty directory for experiments. Never move an initialized root:
Nix indirect garbage-collection roots refer to its original location.
.TP
.BI --source-manifest " FILE"
Initial source pin for update. Used only when no catalog has been saved.
Global options precede the area name.
.SH PORTS ACTIONS
.TP
.BR "update " [ "--manifest FILE" ]
Save a local pinned source and optional curated catalog entries. Without
an explicit manifest, reuse the saved snapshot. Does not refresh channels,
install payloads or change active commands. A manifest contains format = 1
and a [nixpkgs] section with path, revision and system.
.TP
.BI "install " "[NAME ...] [--attribute ATTRIBUTE] [--no-activate]"
Resolve names from the saved snapshot. With no names, install curated entries.
An attribute override requires exactly one name. Record the name/attribute
request for future upgrade. First installation activates defaults unless
--no-activate is given; later installations retain the existing defaults
and existing request target. Reinstallation does not infer legacy intent.
.TP
.BI "upgrade " "[NAME ...]"
Advance recorded attribute requests using the saved catalog snapshot.
With no names, consider all recorded requests. Does not run update or fetch
a newer Nixpkgs snapshot. Retain previous builds and their fixed aliases.
Only active selections following an upgraded request move to its new target;
exact active selections stay pinned. Inactive requests can acquire new builds
without changing defaults. All selected requests form one atomic profile plan.
.PP
.RS
Different versions must be dotted nonnegative integers; numeric downgrades
are refused. An equal version with a different output hash is an eligible
rebuild. Nonnumeric version transitions fail pending a backend version adapter.
These native attribute rules remain in formats 1/2 and for unadopted requests.
Selector mode uses the shared release planner described below. Legacy state has
no recorded requests: installation must explicitly establish native intent.
.RE
.TP
.BI "activate " "IDENTITY_OR_NAME [--follow]"
Select an already installed artifact. An exact identity pins active commands.
A bare name is accepted only when exactly one artifact of that name is
installed; it follows that artifact's recorded request if it is the request's
current target. --follow explicitly follows the request whose current target
is the selected artifact, including when using an exact identity. Fails if
no such request exists. Does not fetch anything.
.TP
.BI "remove " "IDENTITY_OR_NAME ..."
Remove artifacts from the current installation set. Bare names must be
unambiguous. If an active artifact is removed, select the most recently active
remaining artifact, or the most recently installed if none was active.
A removed request target falls back to the most recently active/installed
remaining artifact of its own name and attribute, or its request is removed
if none remains. A fallback only follows a request if it matches its target.
In selector mode, removing a request target removes that request and its series
aliases; no automatic request fallback is inferred. Default fallback still uses
recency and is pinned unless it equals a retained followed request target.
No store objects, old profiles or GC roots are physically deleted.
.TP
.BR "list " [ --exact | --json ]
Show installed versions and active defaults. --exact shows immutable identities
for activation/removal. --json shows detailed installed records.
.TP
.BI "resolve " "SELECTOR --catalog FILE [--json]"
Read-only latest, @series or @=exact-release resolution from a finite
mpm-release-catalog-v1 JSON file. Does not install, activate, adopt a catalog,
refresh sources or migrate state. Source/backend IDs and integrity evidence
are data; no backend command is run. Unknown/preview candidates require an
exact request; unknown version order or ambiguous builds fail.
The same resolver drives selector-mode package operations after explicit metadata adoption.
.TP
.BI "metadata " "--catalog FILE [--adopt SELECTOR ...]"
Explicitly adopt a finite release catalog and format-3 metadata. The catalog is
copied into state; later operations do not need the original file. Without
--adopt, native attribute requests keep their original intent and targets.
With --adopt, record an old-to-new request mapping to a typed selector. The
resolved target must already be installed and match the native request target.
Adoption never imports packages or moves defaults; existing exact defaults stay
pinned. A repeated explicit adoption can accept another installed same-release
build. Plans display compatibility changes; older MPM readers/writers refuse
format 3. Dry-run/cancellation never convert metadata.
.TP
.B status
Print JSON containing catalog, requests, installed records, active selections,
selection policies and recency metadata. A null selection policy is an exact pin.
.TP
.BI "history " "[--json] [--limit N]"
Show committed transactions, newest first; default limit 20, range 1 to 1000.
Records include sequence, UTC timestamp, effective invoking UID, action,
installed/removed identities and before/after catalog, request and active
selection state. Text output summarizes changes; --json includes catalog detail.
Ordering uses sequence, not the wall clock. Legacy profiles without records
end the history chain. This is recovery information, not a tamper-proof audit
log or an undo command.
.TP
.BI "search " "FRAGMENT [--scope ATTRIBUTE] [--limit N] [--json]"
Search attribute names in the saved snapshot. Default limit 50; maximum 200.
.TP
.BI "info " "NAME_OR_IDENTITY [--json]"
Inspect a catalog attribute or exact installed artifact.
.SH SELECTOR MODE
Explicit metadata adoption enables selectors in install, info and activate:
bare NAME selects stable latest; NAME@3.0 selects the newest stable upstream
component prefix [3,0]; NAME@=3.0 selects that exact canonical release, distinct
from 3.0.0. Other version schemes require exact choices. No wildcards, hidden
catalog refresh or source-build fallback. Unsupported/ambiguous ordering fails.
.PP
Install requires explicit selectors in this mode; --attribute remains native
mode only. Distinct latest/series/exact requests coexist. First installation
claims defaults unless --no-activate; later installation retains new artifacts
without moving accepted targets or defaults. Exact requests pin their full build
and dependencies; upgrade advances only eligible series/latest requests.
.PP
Activate resolves from installed release evidence only and downloads nothing.
Historical build preferences do not break installed build ties: use a literal
artifact ID. Exact activation pins defaults; series/latest record a following
request. --follow on a literal ID requires one unambiguous moving request target;
it cannot make an exact-release request follow. Native artifacts without release
evidence retain native activation behavior.
.PP
Each tracked series exports COMMAND-SERIES, e.g. hello-2.12, for every command.
Series aliases follow accepted request targets independently of an exact pinned
default. Fixed aliases keep the full backend identity. Any alias collision or
reserved management name aborts publication. Unknown exports are checked after
import, before publication. Upgrade displays old/new resolution and exact closure
additions/removals; the adapter verifies actual Nix evidence before committing.
Exact requests and older artifacts remain retained, including withdrawn builds.
Same-release replacements need explicit catalog build preference; object hashes
never establish version order. Runtime compatibility is not verified by selection.
.SH MUTATION OPTIONS
update, metadata, install, upgrade, activate and remove accept:
.TP
.B --dry-run
Preview without writing MPM state, GC roots or payloads. Nix evaluation may
populate its own evaluation caches. Unavailable command exports are discovered
only after import; a later collision still aborts profile publication.
.TP
.BR -y , " --yes"
Apply the displayed plan without an interactive confirmation. Does not expand
scope to Base, System, Kernel or another source snapshot.
.SH RECOVERY AND COMPATIBILITY
Mutations are serialized with a lock. A complete profile, state.json and
transaction.json are flushed before atomically switching current. Failures
before the switch preserve the old profile; downloaded objects may remain.
A failure after the switch is reported as possibly committed and the profile
is retained. Complete backend power-loss recovery is not established.
.PP
The first changed operation upgrades format-1 metadata to format 2. Legacy
active selections become exact pins; missing intent is not guessed. Older MPM
writers refuse format 2. Returning to an older System can therefore leave
installed commands usable while its older MPM cannot manage the new state.
Keep a tested reference disk and review the plan's compatibility warning.
Explicit metadata adoption is the only transition to format 3. Read-only and
no-op operations do not convert metadata. There is no reverse conversion.
System rollback does not roll back shared Ports state; use an MPM-capable
System generation after adoption.
.PP
Fixed aliases include the command, version and full Nix output hash.
After changing command availability, the current shell may need
.BR "hash -r" .
There is no general undo, pruning/GC, production project lock or automatic
reconciliation of direct Nix changes.
.SH RELATION TO SYSTEM UPGRADE
mpm system upgrade is accepted design, not an implemented command. It must
plan against the selected deployment, distinguish selected from running
content, and publish a new immutable System generation for next boot.
Ports upgrade changes the current shared application profile immediately.
Neither operation may silently change the other area's requests, catalog or
activation, nor Base or Kernel selections. Sharing immutable Nix objects
does not merge those policies. A future System updater must check whether
its bundled MPM can read/write the selected persistent metadata format;
mutable account, home and application state is not automatically rolled back.
.SH LOCAL DISCOVERY PROTOTYPE
The repository frontend supports catalog search, show and versions with an
explicit local --catalog discovery envelope. Area-scoped forms include
ports search, ports show and ports versions with that option. Ordinary Ports
search without a discovery catalog retains its existing Nix source semantics.
The current installed System launcher still invokes the Nix prototype;
run local discovery through python3 -B tools/mpm in a checkout.
.PP
Discovery search matches literal names and declared aliases, case-insensitively.
Use --exact for a whole name/alias, --description to additionally search
descriptions, and --json for structured search output. There is no implicit
regexp, network access or installed-state adoption. One row groups a package's
builds; source evidence remains visible.
.PP
The read-only kernel resolve command requires --catalog, --base-context and
--policy pinned, latest-lts or latest-upstream. Pinned takes --series major.minor
or --release exact-release. All policies apply declared selected-Base limits;
an impossible request fails without a policy or Base fallback. This does not
install a kernel, persist its policy or change boot selection. See
docs/mpm-catalog.md for synthetic examples and the experimental schema.
.SH LOCAL KERNEL INSTALL AND STATUS
The repository frontend supports kernel install --from-file ARCHIVE
--expected-id sha256:FULL_HEX_ID, with optional --dry-run or -y, and kernel
status with optional --verify and --json. Use --root DIRECTORY before kernel
or after the action, once only. Default registry: /var/lib/mojt/kernel;
shared mutations require an administrator. A private user-owned root is supported.
.PP
Installation retains a verified archive, private read-only Kernel tree and
installation record by exact artifact ID. Repeating it checks retained content
and makes no change. Unsupported dependencies and requirements are rejected.
Status distinguishes installed content from unknown selected deployment and
the actual running release; uname alone does not establish artifact identity.
No boot selection or running module tree changes. Deployment compatibility is
unevaluated. See docs/mpm-kernel.md for the supported local subset and limits.
.SH READ-ONLY KERNEL DEPLOYMENT PLAN
kernel plan --request FILE [--json] checks a complete explicit local plan.
Use --root DIRECTORY before kernel or after plan, once only, to name the Kernel
registry. No registry, lock, selection, cache or boot files are created.
.PP
The exact candidate must be installed and pass a fresh integrity scrub.
The request binds selected Base and System identities, Base Kernel limits,
System ABI/state/Ports/shell facts and an initramfs checksum/binding to the exact
Kernel image/module inventory, filesystem UUID and console. Base/System userspace
must remain selected unchanged; missing or incompatible evidence fails.
No running release, current link, provider or alternate Base is used as fallback.
.PP
Successful plans still report boot_ready false. Explicit metadata is not an
authenticated disk selection; Base/System payloads, actual initramfs contents,
hardware/firmware, Secure Boot and runtime ABI remain unaudited. The planner
does not generate initramfs, write GRUB or modify selected/running state.
See docs/kernel-deployment-plan.md for strict experimental schemas and limits.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /var/lib/mojt/ports/current
Atomic selection of an internal retained profile.
.TP
.B /var/lib/mojt/ports/profiles/*/state.json
Authoritative snapshot of Ports metadata for that profile.
.TP
.B /var/lib/mojt/ports/profiles/*/transaction.json
Transaction and ancestry, published with its snapshot. No separate database.
.TP
.B /var/lib/mojt/ports/gcroots
Retained Nix source and payload roots.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mpm ports resolve hello@2.12 --catalog hello-releases.json
mpm ports metadata --catalog hello-releases.json --dry-run
mpm ports metadata --catalog hello-releases.json -y
mpm ports install hello@2.12 -y
mpm ports activate hello@2.12 -y

# Native attribute workflow on an unconverted state root:
mpm ports update --dry-run
mpm ports update -y
mpm ports install hello -y
mpm ports upgrade hello --dry-run
mpm ports upgrade hello -y
mpm ports list --exact
mpm ports activate 'IDENTITY_FROM_LIST' -y
mpm ports activate 'CURRENT_REQUEST_TARGET_FROM_LIST' --follow -y
mpm ports history
man mpm
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, cancellation or an unchanged plan; nonzero on failure.
Shared Ports changes require an administrator in the Mojt account-enabled
System. Ordinary users may list, inspect status, read history and resolve a
readable catalog.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nix-store (1),
.BR man (1)
.PP
